# Append-only documents. Every pull request adds an entry at the top of these
# two, at the same anchor, so any two open branches conflict by construction —
# it happened three times in a fortnight and the resolution was identical each
# time: keep both.
#
# merge=union does exactly that — but only for merges made with git itself.
# GitHub's web conflict editor does not honour it: these files still arrive
# there as conflicts, and choosing a side drops the other. That is how #106's
# readme line was lost (restored in #108). So a pull request that conflicts is
# updated locally — `git merge origin/main`, then `composer make-pot` — and
# pushed clean, rather than resolved in the browser.
#
# The cost is that git stops reporting a conflict here, so two branches editing
# the SAME existing line get both versions silently rather than a marker. These
# files are almost entirely append-only, which is what makes the trade worth
# taking; the one line that is genuinely edited rather than appended is
# readme.txt's "Stable tag", and bin/validate-config.php fails the build if
# union merge ever duplicates a single-value header there.
CHANGELOG.md    merge=union
readme.txt      merge=union

# Deliberately NOT union: languages/producerkit.pot is generated, and
# concatenating two versions of it produces a file neither branch would
# reproduce. The right resolution is always to regenerate, and
# `composer make-pot:check` fails CI on a stale one.
